:"e0cec4cbe8ef9c0641ed7459","time_limit":25},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What are the eigenvalues of a triangular matrix?","options":["its diagonal entries","its off-diagonal entries","its row sums","always $0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"73e52e3ff2b6edd496697e3d","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&5\\end{pmatrix}$ and $p=(1,0)$, which statement is correct?","options":["$p$ is not an eigenvector because the eigenvalue is zero.","$p$ is an eigenvector with eigenvalue $0$.","$p$ is the zero vector.","$p$ is an eigenvector with eigenvalue $5$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2948eda69055e349a61aa6c6","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $p$ is an eigenvector for $\\lambda$, what about $cp$ for $c\\neq0$?","options":["$cp$ is never an eigenvector.","$cp$ is also an eigenvector for the same $\\lambda$.","$cp$ is an eigenvector only when $c=1$.","$cp$ has eigenvalue $c\\lambda$ automatically."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b05582ceee7c517b3a8debbe","time_limit":35},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the eigenspace for an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ built from?","options":["All vectors in the codomain regardless of $A$","The solutions of $Ap=p+\\lambda$","The nonzero solutions of $(A-\\lambda I)p=0$, plus zero","Only the diagonal entries of $A$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"aaffe76517bc6fdaefb3de56","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=\\begin{pmatrix}3&1\\\\0&3\\end{pmatrix}$, which vectors are eigenvectors for $\\lambda=3$?","options":["Only the zero vector","All nonzero vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2$","Nonzero multiples of $(0,1)$","Nonzero multiples of $(1,0)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fb970f91bd749bba79a277a0","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Over the real numbers, what happens for $A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$?","options":["It has no real eigenvectors.","Every nonzero real vector is an eigenvector.","It has eigenvalue $0$ with eigenvector $(0,1)$.","It has eigenvalue $1$ with eigenvector $(1,0)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9d6b407350003c209f3cac92","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_003_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{p}=-2\\boldsymbol{p}$ for a nonzero real vector $\\boldsymbol{p}$. What does $\\boldsymbol{A}$ do to $\\boldsymbol{p}$?","options":["It reverses $\\boldsymbol{p}$ and doubles its length","It keeps the direction of $\\boldsymbol{p}$ and doubles its length","It turns $\\boldsymbol{p}$ into a perpendicular vector","It sends $\\boldsymbol{p}$ to the zero vector"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ef8d14617642e9b41bdf76db","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_015_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, which statement about the vectors $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ is true?","options":["$(1,1)$ has eigenvalue $1$ and $(1,-1)$ has eigenvalue $-1$","$(1,1)$ has eigenvalue $-1$ and $(1,-1)$ has eigenvalue $1$","$(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ both have eigenvalue $1$","Only $(1,1)$ is an eigenvector; $(1,-1)$ is not"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a6d5ab5dfda9a7f64ab69dbb","time_limit":40}]}}